    What parser.py does:
    - parse_csv(text, source_hint=None) -> (list[SaleRecord], list[str]); returns (records, warnings), never raises.
    - Auto-detects source from header signatures: "order id"+"order date/status" -> payhip, "sale id"+"created at/refunded" -> gumroad, else generic. source_hint overrides; an invalid hint is ignored with a warning.
    - Synonym-based header matching: headers normalized to lowercase, punctuation stripped, whitespace collapsed (e.g. "ORDER  DATE" -> "order date", "price_cents" -> "price cents"), mapped to fields via priority-ordered synonym lists. "Price Cents" columns auto-divide by 100.
    - Dates parsed via fromisoformat + 28 strptime formats (ISO, US m/d/Y, human "Jul 2, 2026 2:05:11 PM", EU d.m.Y). Naive datetimes normalized (strips UTC/GMT/Z).
    - Bad rows (bad/missing date, unparseable price, missing product) -> warning + skip. Bad qty -> warn + default 1. Missing optional columns (qty/email/currency/order_id/question) -> model defaults, no warning.
    - Refund detection: refunded/refund-status column values (yes/true/1/refunded/full/partial, or any non-empty value like a refund date), status column containing "refund", and negative prices all set refunded=True.
    - Question capture: buyer question/notes/message columns -> record.question.
    
    One fix along the way: the payhip fixture's human date "Jul 2, 2026 2:05:11 PM" contains a comma, so it had to be quoted in the CSV (as real Payhip exports do) — that was a fixture bug, not a parser bug.
    
    core/models.py untouched; no files created outside the assignment.
